Context (from the neighbouring pages): Grothendieck is classifying covers X' of a space or stack X, given by a fibre E at the base point x0 with an action of pi = pi_1(X, x0). E carries a structure of torsor under a group M, which is evidently abelian here. Writing Aff(E) = M ⋊ Aut(M) only makes sense in that case. He wants to extend the action of pi on E to an action of a group M~, which sits in an extension 1 -> M -> M~ -> Gamma -> 1, with Gamma acting on M through theta_Gamma: Gamma -> Aut(M).

The previous page proves formula (280): g(lambda·x) = gamma_M(lambda)·(gx). So any element of M~ acts on E by an affine transformation of the M-torseur E, with linear part given by the image of g in Gamma.

This page draws the consequence as diagram (281), a morphism of short exact sequences:
- top row: 1 -> M -> M~ -> Gamma -> 1;
- bottom row: 1 -> M -> Aff(E) -> Aut(M) -> 1;
- vertical maps: id_M on the left, theta_Gamma on the right (both known), and an unknown middle map theta_{M~}: M~ -> Aff(E).

Claim: the category of the sought X' (with Gamma-action satisfying the conditions) is equivalent to the category of pairs (E, theta_{M~}). Here E is an M-torseur and theta_{M~} is a group homomorphism M~ -> Aff(E) making (281) commute.

Once E is fixed (unique up to non-unique isomorphism), Aff(E) is an extension of Aut(M) by M. It is split, by the points of E (this is the marginal note on the next page). Pulling it back along theta_Gamma gives a split extension of Gamma by M, namely M ⋊ Gamma. Commuting middle arrows are then the same as isomorphisms of extensions M~ ≅ M ⋊ Gamma, i.e. splittings of M~.

The next page concludes:
- such an arrow exists iff the class of M~ in H^2(Gamma, M) vanishes;
- the arrows then form a torsor under Z^1(Gamma, M), the crossed homomorphisms;
- M-conjugacy classes of arrows are in bijection with H^1(Gamma, M).

Purpose: a step in the Longue Marche analysis of covers and fundamental-group extensions, reducing a lifting problem to standard group cohomology.
